## Original family admission and implementation boundary · `sec:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-proposals`

**Decision (Finite family admission revision)** · `dec:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-admission`

Use the admission manifest in (`tab:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-manifest`) for the parameterized implementation, including independent timing combined with shared clock and phase offsets. These are design contracts, not claims of registered implementation capabilities. Original-source substitution and finite topology have separate checkable schemas so that calibration adapters cannot be composed without a joint proof. The implementation cost is additional CAS schemas, receiving-store encodings and bindings restoration checks, plus charged fallback for excluded combinations. This decision bounds acceleration, not the original question grammar or candidate domain. The family claim may close with the excluded combinations on charged exact fallback, subject to (`dec:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-evidence`); accelerated family laws for those combinations are not required for that claim. The finite timing/clock/phase lift is admitted because its added coordinates preserve affine elimination and complete reconstruction. The broader supported scalar fragment in (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-fibre-recipe`) retains every original constraint on fallback. No implementation standing changes merely from this admission contract.

**Definition (Original substitution and ordered family binders)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-binders`

Within the adopted manifest, retain `lc-positive-root-composition@1` unchanged under (`dec:nrz:cas-lc-root-composition`); do not broaden its free-input wire format. A distinct `lc-source-substitution@1` theorem composes it with an original source DAG and checked simultaneous substitution. Its inventory contains the original context, ordered declarations including repetitions, every original constraint, source-domain predicates, conditional maps, target DAGs and the positional substitution map. A target such as L(s)*C(s) remains that original target after substitution; arrived enclosures witness its premises and never create replacement free physical inputs. General source expressions may supply conditional coverage only when their ordered native operation laws establish it; feasibility still needs reconstruction. Equality of numerical values does not identify sources.

Order the closed coverage statement as `forall p, forall b in B(p), forall s in S(p,b): C(p,b,s) implies F(p,b,s) in E(p,b)`. Here p is the declared parameter inventory in first-occurrence order, followed by the six root endpoints in their existing order, then declared enclosure/stratum parameters; b is the exact ordered integer lift tuple; s follows original source declaration order. The full inventory retains duplicate positions even where binder identity is shared. Bounds depending on earlier variables are conditional domains, never reordered independent boxes. An image converse is a separate statement `forall p, forall b in B(p), forall y in I(p,b), exists s in S(p,b): C(p,b,s) and F(p,b,s)=y`, carrying an explicit reconstruction map. Nonemptiness has its own witness; an empty antecedent can prove coverage but not existence. A source common to several samples occurs once outside their conjunction, so pointwise witnesses cannot discharge `exists s, forall j`. No arbitrary alternation, interchange of source/index quantifiers or general existential elimination is admitted by these schemas.

