**Definition (Finite face and lift certificate revision one)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-faces`

The `lc-affine-fibre@1` certificate records the complete original inventory and selected tag, exact ordered lift domain and its independent completeness proof, ordered coordinates, ordered original inequality rows, strictness/ownership bits, projection/elimination steps, and reconstruction formulas. Coefficients are exact rational expressions in the retained pi constant where integer lifts require it; they are not rational approximations to pi. Every division has an exact nonzero sign premise and every comparison uses checked pi evidence or exact equality. If these predicates cannot be settled, no face topology is certified. The admitted physical supports are closed and bounded with rate separated from zero; strict faces occur in partition ownership and are recorded separately. Open physical supports use fallback in this revision.

For `timing-clock-phase`, retain one w_j per original observation beside the common z. The measured lifted phase is `gamma+a*r_j+w_j`; the physical lifted phase is `gamma+s+a*r_j+w_j`. A physical timestamp restriction `T_j_min<=r_j+d+delta_j<=T_j_max` becomes `a*(T_j_min-r_j)<=z+w_j<=a*(T_j_max-r_j)` because a>0. Keep these rows, the error bounds `a*delta_j_min<=w_j<=a*delta_j_max`, every measured/physical phase strip and all shared-offset restrictions in the same lifted polytope. Every repeated original timestamp or observation adds its own row. For each fixed parameter vector these are affine in the lifted coordinates; their coefficients remain symbolic functions of the arrived supports and reported times. Eliminating w_j must retain both projection implications and reconstruction, not just the familiar outer timing strip.

Encode each projected polygon as an ordered vertex list with active original-row IDs, an ordered edge list with endpoint vertex IDs and supporting-row IDs, and dimension tag empty/point/segment/polygon. Attach elimination implications in both directions and a lift into the original lifted polytope for each projected vertex. The independent checker reconstructs every intersection of independent boundary rows, checks feasibility against all rows, accounts for duplicates, collinear edges and rank degeneracies, and compares the complete resulting vertex/edge incidence with the proposal. Boundedness and exhaustive elimination are premises; checking only the submitted vertices is insufficient. For point and segment tags, affine-hull equations and both endpoints replace polygon orientation. The parameter stratum fixes determinant signs, row order comparisons, feasibility and incidence; an equality has its own stratum. Changing any of them requires another certificate or fallback. This is a finite schema indexed by the complete row inventory, not permission to cache arbitrary arrived coefficients as a theorem.

The varying parameters are the arrived lower/upper timestamp and measured/physical lifted-phase supports, positive-rate bounds, initial-phase bounds and, for `clock-phase` or `timing-clock-phase`, the common clock and phase-offset bounds; the combined tag also retains every independent timestamp-error bound; exact reported times may also vary as declared parameters. Their identities and source/companion maps are fixed by the original question. The exact branch tuple, row inventory and pi authority are retained theorem inputs; a changed branch inventory or stronger pi certificate needs the appropriate exact authority, not an untyped floating parameter. Rows and vertex coordinates are symbolic functions of these parameters. The certificate proves their incidence for every parameter vector q in one stratum, rather than recording a new exact coefficient list for each arrival.

Encode that stratum as a finite conjunction `h_k(q)>0`, `h_k(q)>=0` or `h_k(q)=0`, with each h_k an ordered native-origin expression. Its inventory contains support-order and rate-separation predicates; determinant signs for the chosen intersections; signs of each original-row residual at each candidate intersection; duplicate/collinearity, orientation and edge-order predicates; and denominator signs for elimination and reconstruction. Rational expressions may be cleared to polynomial sign predicates only with the denominator signs retained, so the checker preserves inequality direction. Coefficients retain exact pi references where needed. A live arrival authenticates all parameter origins and evaluates outward enclosures of every h_k; (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-containment`) checks each against its required sign stratum. Equality needs exact evidence unless an authenticated singleton enclosure proves zero. The finite conjunction passes only when every predicate passes on the same current source/version closure. An inconclusive predicate uses charged exact fallback or retains the unresolved outcome; a different sign pattern selects another certified stratum. Thus the reusable theorem and symbolic incidence remain cached across arrivals, while membership scans and new attachments remain charged application work under (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-fibre-recipe`).

Forward inclusion follows by elimination of the original affine inequalities. Conversely, a convex combination of projected vertices lifts by the same weights to a point satisfying every lifted affine row. For `clock-phase` and `timing-clock-phase`, positive a permits `d=z/a` and `alpha=gamma+s-z`; the combined tag additionally restores `delta_j=w_j/a` and `t_j=r_j+d+delta_j` for every j. The same lifted point satisfies every original measured/physical phase strip, timestamp restriction, independent error interval and shared offset, so restoration cannot select incompatible marginal witnesses. For `timing`, each independent timestamp is selected from the nonempty intersection specified in (`alg:nrz:lc-evaluation-recovery-graph`). Thus the certificate can establish image equality and conditional witnesses, not just endpoint coverage. Each integer lift branch has its own certificate; a finite rendered selection is not a proof that the original integer domain is finite. Weak completeness evidence retains that full domain under (`rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-fibre-barrier`).

**Definition (Future ray, reference and union certificate revision one)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-futures`

For each admitted future tag, retain the complete original fibre certificate, projected `(a,c)` polygon (c=alpha for actual time and c=beta for reported time), reference coordinate/epoch, and exact rational reference R. Supply vertex IDs v_low and v_high plus, for every vertex v, the comparisons `a_v-a_low>=0`, `(c_v-c_low)+(a_v-a_low)*R>=0`, `a_high-a_v>=0` and `(c_high-c_v)+(a_high-a_v)*R>=0`. The corresponding affine differences remain nonnegative for every t>=R. These finite inequalities therefore prove the selected lines extremal over the whole ray. R also satisfies the original safe-reference premises and lies after the observation window in the declared coordinate; an unsatisfied or undecidable comparison uses fallback. No floating timestamp or last-arrival index encodes the reference. Parallel lines and zero width retain their exact equality cases. An irrational safe reference may be replaced only by a proved later rational reference with the narrower ray explicitly requested; it cannot silently answer the original earlier-ray question.

For `timing-clock-phase`, both actual-time and reported-time futures project the complete lifted polytope, including every w_j and its original constraints, to `(a,alpha)` or `(a,beta)` respectively. Vertex lifts and interpolation restore each original observation, timestamp, delta_j and the common d and s through (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-faces`). The future query introduces no new independent timestamp error. The `broad` closed-form speed-up keeps its stated `clock-phase` scope; the combined timing case uses the full projection and the same ray inequalities.

The ray certificate is parameterized by the same arrived support vector q and, if declared varying, the exact query reference R. Its retained vertex IDs name symbolic vertex functions `(a_v(q),c_v(q))`, not coefficients frozen at one arrival. Add the four per-vertex ray differences above, observation-window separation and all reference premises to the finite sign conjunction in (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-faces`). Clear denominators only under their retained signs. At each arrival apply the native-origin and containment law to every resulting expression, or use charged exact fallback; only the conjunction authorizes reuse of the fixed extremizer theorem for that arrival and reference. Reusing the theorem therefore needs no per-arrival CAS derivation when the authenticated parameters stay in its stratum, but a new scoped application and the full current membership check are still required.

For any fixed t>=R, the projected polygon is convex and its affine image is the interval between the two extrema. Interpolating their lifted vertices realizes every value in that interval; restoration in (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-faces`) gives original physical witnesses. The horizon checker applies the existing exact full-turn width law with its own pi authority, strict faces and finite/infinite/undecided outcome. The witness is a map depending on t and the requested image value, not one fixed physical source asserted to realize every future value.

The union encoding admits a singleton branch or a finite, complete list of gauge-equivalent branches only. It contains ordered branch IDs, complete lift-domain evidence, a representative ID, exact integer full-turn shifts and bidirectional original witness maps preserving wrapped phase and all source restrictions. A checker verifies these maps against each original branch, rather than comparing rounded endpoint widths. All other finite unions and infinite non-gauge alias families retain their complete domain and unresolved whole-family horizon, even when branch horizons are individually certified. In particular taking a minimum or maximum of branch thresholds, or convexifying disconnected images, is not an admitted union witness under (`rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-horizon-barrier`).

**Decision (Native containment arithmetic revision)** · `dec:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-arithmetic`

Use the two containment contracts `lc-containment-binary32@1` and `lc-containment-binary64@1`, distinct from the existing root arithmetic revision. They authenticate the exact import, error scope and comparison schedule needed by (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-membership-recipe`). Their implementation requires two independent arithmetic proofs and adversarial fixtures, new origin/application identity fields and rejection or exact fallback on unsupported arithmetic modes. This selects no Work tariff or scalar approximation implementation. The contracts below are mathematical specifications, not claims that the existing omega implementation already satisfies composed-error admission.

**Table (Native decoding contracts)** · `tab:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-formats`

|Contract|Exact finite decoding and arithmetic assumptions|Nonadmission|
|--------|------------------------------------------------|------------|
|`lc-containment-binary32@1`|IEEE binary32, precision 24, normal unbiased exponents -126 through 127, subnormal quantum 2^-149; signed zeros decode to zero but retain their bits in provenance.|NaN, infinite physical endpoints, overflow without finite outward bounds, flush-to-zero or denormals-are-zero arithmetic.|
|`lc-containment-binary64@1`|IEEE binary64, precision 53, normal unbiased exponents -1022 through 1023, subnormal quantum 2^-1074; the same zero convention.|The same exclusions, checked independently in binary64.|

Both contracts use correctly rounded round-to-nearest ties-to-even primitives with gradual underflow and no unrecorded contraction, reassociation or extended intermediate precision. Outward operations use a proved directed implementation, or the predecessor/successor of a correctly rounded finite result when that supplies a finite bound. Exact conversion of a rational threshold is independently checked against its decoded dyadic value; casting a decimal is not a proof. Square root and reciprocal have explicit positive-domain premises. An operation without the required rounding theorem is outside this arithmetic revision even if its outputs pass sampled tests.

**Definition (Directed containment and error composition argument)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-containment`

For either format let D decode a finite bit pattern to its exact real dyadic. A current authenticated enclosure [l,u] must first prove `D(l)<=F(s)<=D(u)` for all original admissible sources. For an exact stratum [A,B], prepare inward thresholds a=ceil_format(A), b=floor_format(B), with checked `A<=D(a)` and `D(b)<=B`. Accept a closed lower face only if `l>=a`, a closed upper face only if `u<=b`. For an open lower face use `l>a`, or accept `l>=a` when the import proof checks `A<D(a)` strictly; for an open upper face use `u<b`, or accept `u<=b` when it checks `D(b)<B` strictly. Ordered finite IEEE comparison agrees with real order, including equal signed zeros. Thus `A<=D(a)<=D(l)<=F(s)<=D(u)<=D(b)<=B`, with strict transitivity on every open face. Strictness follows either from native separation or from the strictly inward threshold, so an unrepresentable face need not lose the equality-to-threshold case. A one-sided stratum such as (0,infinity) has no face on its infinite side: apply only the finite-face test, with the finite-endpoint enclosure premise already bounding the other side. No infinity is imported as a finite threshold. Reject inverted enclosures, unordered comparisons or absent finite inward thresholds for an actual finite face before applying the law. Equality requires separate exact evidence unless an authenticated singleton enclosure proves the required exact value, including zero for the sign-predicate conjunction. This proof authenticates a new current application only with the original value, companion, selection and version closure from (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-attachment`).

For a center c and nonnegative total radius T, compute l by outward subtraction of T and u by outward addition, never by nearest subtraction/addition alone. T already contains numerical error once under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-error-composition`); do not add the numerical companion again. More generally for an operation g and computed center c_out, propagate each input's total interval to an outward interval J_T containing g on the full original support, then choose an upward bound on `max(abs(c_out-J_T.lower),abs(J_T.upper-c_out))` as T_out. This includes current rounding because c_out is compared with the mathematical range, and includes previous numerical uncertainty through J_T. Domain signs and all interval arithmetic are proved outward. Correlation may make J_T conservative; it cannot establish an existence converse.

A composed numerical-only radius needs a separately defined exact nominal evaluation on the same original graph and reading maps. If input nominal values lie in [c-N,c+N], propagate those intervals through g to J_N and bound their distance from c_out in the same way to obtain N_out. A local-operation radius instead bounds only `abs(c_out-g(c_1,...,c_k))` at represented operands. It cannot replace N_out when incoming N is nonzero. For example, for multiplication an upper numerical bound is `abs(c_1)*N_2+abs(c_2)*N_1+N_1*N_2+rho_local`, all additions/products upward enclosed. Here rho_local includes the absolute underflow term: for a finite correctly rounded operation, `abs(fl(x)-x)<=u*abs(x)+eta`, with u=2^-24 and eta=2^-150 in binary32, or u=2^-53 and eta=2^-1075 in binary64. These are exact mathematical bounds; eta must not be rounded down to zero when represented in a numerical bound. The analogous total bound uses T_1,T_2 and includes rho_local once; it does not add N_out on top. Root and reciprocal use their interval ranges on positive, zero-excluding domains rather than a linear derivative guess. Missing nominal lineage keeps the numerical companion LocalOperation and refuses a composed numerical-only claim; it does not invalidate a separately established total outer cover. Independent format fixtures include subnormals, signed zero, extreme finite magnitudes, exact and adjacent open faces, unchanged-center companion mutations, and nonzero inherited numerical error.

**Decision (Family cache ownership)** · `dec:nrz:lc-evaluation-family-cache`

Family closure waits for the generic exact cache under (`rule:nrz:backlog-lc-order`). Add no LC-only cache and use the ownership split below, preserving the root replay contract: an existing durable judgment is not a charged authenticated second-preparation path. Cold schema development can proceed independently, but closure waits for generic lookup/citation, admission-history and literal boundary evidence. CAS owns immutable exact schema/claim/proof closures and authenticated store entries; the evaluation owner owns bounded warm references, bindings owns correspondence and live application evidence, and matrix owns numerical occupations. No heap value enters an exact cache key.

